  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Translate the LTI input-output convolution relation $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$ into $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ and identify $X(s)$, $H(s)$, and $Y(s)$ as the input, system, and output transforms.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When an LTI output is written as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, the cue is the convolution symbol $*$. That cue tells you to move from the time-domain relation to the Laplace-domain product $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $X(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}$ for the input transform, $H(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{h(t)\\}$ for the system transform, and $Y(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}$ for the output transform. Keep those roles fixed whenever you read or write the relation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)*h(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{h(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substituting those symbols into the transform rule gives $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. In this form, the output transform is the product of the input transform and the system transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the direct mapping you will reuse in the next steps: start from $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, identify $X(s)$, $H(s)$, and $Y(s)$, and read the output as the product relation in the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Translating an LTI Convolution Output into a Product Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Show that from $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, with $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\frac{2}{s+2}$, the output transform is $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\frac{2}{(s+1)(s+2)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the convolution symbol in $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$. For an LTI system, that cue tells you to use the Laplace convolution theorem and move from the time-domain relation to the product relation in the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)*h(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{h(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now keep the symbol roles fixed: $X(s)$ is the input transform, $H(s)$ is the system transform, and $Y(s)$ is the output transform. With that mapping in place, the identity becomes $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot\\frac{2}{s+2}=\\frac{2}{(s+1)(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the worked substitution: the time-domain convolution becomes multiplication, the input and system transforms stay on the right side, and the result is the output transform $Y(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying $X(s)$, $H(s)$, and $Y(s)$ in the Product Relation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $Y(s)=\\frac{4}{(s+1)(s+2)}$ from an LTI relation, identify which symbolic factor is $X(s)$, which is $H(s)$, and which is $Y(s)$, then state the interpretation in one sentence.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the left side: by definition, the quantity labeled $Y(s)$ is the output transform. The factorization on the right side must therefore be read as the product of the input transform and the system transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{4}{(s+1)(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the LTI interpretation to assign the fixed system factor to $H(s)$ and the remaining factor to $X(s)$. In a product relation, the two right-hand factors are not interchangeable: their roles come from the system statement, not from algebra alone.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s),\\qquad X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+1},\\qquad H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the interpretation is: $Y(s)$ is the output transform, $X(s)$ is the input transform, and $H(s)$ is the system transform. This is the role check you will reuse whenever a product in the $s$-domain appears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing $Y(s)$ from Given Input and System Transforms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $Y(s)$ from $X(s)=\\frac{3}{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\frac{s}{s+4}$ by applying the topic core move.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product relation $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. That tells you to keep the input transform and system transform separate and multiply them directly in the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given transforms exactly as written: $X(s)=\\frac{3}{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\frac{s}{s+4}$. Then multiply the numerators and denominators without changing the symbol roles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{3}{s+1}\\cdot\\frac{s}{s+4}=\\frac{3s}{(s+1)(s+4)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output transform is $Y(s)=\\frac{3s}{(s+1)(s+4)}$. The computation stays inside the same interpretation: $X(s)$ is the input transform, $H(s)$ is the system transform, and $Y(s)$ is the output transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "$Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ as the Laplace-Domain LTI System Relation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "With fixed $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}$ and varying input transforms, compare outputs for $X_1(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}$ and $X_2(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}$ using the same $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ relation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $H(s)$ is fixed. That means the system part stays the same while the input transform changes, so you compare outputs by applying the same product rule to each input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y_1(s)=X_1(s)H(s), \\qquad Y_2(s)=X_2(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given transforms exactly as written: $X_1(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}$, $X_2(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}$, and $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}$. The only change across the two cases is the input factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y_1(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s+2}=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}, \\qquad Y_2(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s+2}=\\frac{2}{(s+1)(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the same Laplace-domain system relation produces outputs that differ only through the input transform. The fixed factor is $H(s)$, and each output is still an $s$-domain result of the form $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
